1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
We are ZENITH LABS. I am Abed El Rahmane
Jaafir.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:11,000
And I am Mohamad Alayli. This is ParallaX,
for the challenge Planet X and SPHEREx.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,791
Pluto was found by blinking two
photographs. The challenge asks for a tool

4
00:00:16,791 --> 00:00:20,000
to do that with the SPHEREx infrared sky.

5
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:26,319
But SPHEREx never photographs a spot in
the same colour twice, and its pixels are

6
00:00:26,319 --> 00:00:31,000
coarse. Blink two dates, and stars light
up as false alarms.

7
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:36,500
So we chose to correct the colour first,
and only then compare.

8
00:00:36,500 --> 00:00:42,558
Our data is open: SPHEREx images from the
NASA/IPAC archive, with Gaia, CatWISE, JPL

9
00:00:42,558 --> 00:00:44,000
Horizons and SkyBoT.

10
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:50,094
A Python pipeline does the science, and a
React and TypeScript app shows it. An

11
00:00:50,094 --> 00:00:55,571
index of more than 1.6 million public
images lets the browser fetch any

12
00:00:55,571 --> 00:00:57,500
position, with no server.

13
00:00:57,500 --> 00:01:03,000
Here is Barnard’s Star, blinked pass by
pass: you can see it move.

14
00:01:03,000 --> 00:01:07,836
Here is one frame differenced three ways:
naive, wavelength-matched, and our

15
00:01:07,836 --> 00:01:12,673
chromatic template, which predicts the
frame at its own wavelengths from the

16
00:01:12,673 --> 00:01:13,500
other passes.

17
00:01:13,500 --> 00:01:19,309
Click any star for its spectrum. And here
an asteroid crosses, right under its

18
00:01:19,309 --> 00:01:20,500
predicted label.

19
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:25,445
What is distinctive: we publish the
measurements. On blank sky, at the same

20
00:01:25,445 --> 00:01:30,456
false-alarm rate, the template finds
moving sources 1.2 to 1.9 times fainter

21
00:01:30,456 --> 00:01:32,500
than a wavelength-matched pair.

22
00:01:32,500 --> 00:01:38,080
We started on the twelfth and thirteenth
of November, before the on-site event, as

23
00:01:38,080 --> 00:01:43,388
the organizers allowed. An SI coding
assistant helped; we directed and checked

24
00:01:43,388 --> 00:01:44,000
its work.

25
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:49,890
The hard part was honesty. Bright stars
saturate, parallax is not measured, and

26
00:01:49,890 --> 00:01:52,500
this is not a Planet Nine detector.

27
00:01:52,500 --> 00:01:55,000
ParallaX is open source. Thank you.

